  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "Shifted Impulses",
    "lesson_core_move": "Locate a shifted impulse by reading the time value that makes its argument zero and carrying its area weight in continuous-time impulse expressions.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Shifted Impulses",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Shifted Impulses",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A shifted impulse is still the unit impulse, but its location is wherever the inside of $\\delta(\\cdot)$ becomes zero. Read the argument first, then solve for the time value that activates the impulse.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\delta(t)=\\delta(t-0)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the base case $\\delta(t)$. Because the argument is just $t$, the zero occurs at $t=0$, so this impulse is located at the origin.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t-t_0=0 \\Rightarrow t=t_0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For a form like $\\delta(t-t_0)$, set the argument equal to zero: $t-t_0=0$. Solving gives $t=t_0$, so a positive $t_0$ means the impulse is delayed to the right.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t+t_0=0 \\Rightarrow t=-t_0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $\\delta(t+t_0)$, do the same thing: $t+t_0=0$. Solving gives $t=-t_0$, so the impulse is advanced to the left.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Keep the coefficient with the shifted impulse as its area weight. The location comes from the zero of the argument; the coefficient tells you how much impulse area sits at that same location.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Shifted Impulses",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Locating an Impulse from $\\delta$(t - t0)",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Locate the shifted impulse in $\\delta(t-3)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the shifted impulse $\\delta(t-3)$. To locate it, set the internal argument equal to zero and solve for the time that activates the impulse.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t-3=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Solving gives $t=3$, so the impulse is activated at time $3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t=3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So $\\delta(t-3)$ is a right-shifted impulse located at $t=3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Shifted Impulses",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Locating an Impulse from $\\delta$(t + t0)",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Locate the impulse in $\\delta(t+2)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the shifted impulse $\\delta(t+2)$. To locate it, set the internal argument equal to zero and solve for the time that activates the impulse.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t+2=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Solving gives $t=-2$, so the impulse is activated at time $-2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t=-2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So $\\delta(t+2)$ is an advanced impulse located at $t=-2$. The coefficient is $1$, so the impulse keeps unit area.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Shifted Impulses",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Carrying the Area Weight of a Shifted Impulse",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find location and area of $5\\delta(t-2.5)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the shifted impulse $5\\delta(t-2.5)$. First locate the impulse by setting the internal argument equal to zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t-2.5=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Solving gives $t=2.5$, so the impulse is activated at time $2.5$. The coefficient $5$ is the area weight and stays attached to the impulse.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t=2.5",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So $5\\delta(t-2.5)$ is a shifted impulse located at $t=2.5$ with area $5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Shifted Impulses",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Reading Sums of Shifted Impulses",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Interpret $\\delta(t-3)-\\delta(t+1)+4\\delta(t-0.5)$ by enumerating each impulse term.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a sum of shifted impulses. Read each term separately: the zero of each argument gives the time, and the coefficient gives the area.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\delta(t-3)-\\delta(t+1)+4\\delta(t-0.5)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $\\delta(t-3)$, solve $t-3=0$ to get $t=3$, with area $1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $-\\delta(t+1)$, solve $t+1=0$ to get $t=-1$, with area $-1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $4\\delta(t-0.5)$, solve $t-0.5=0$ to get $t=0.5$, with area $4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the complete ordered list is $(3,1)$, $(-1,-1)$, and $(0.5,4)$. Each shifted impulse is listed once and kept separate.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
